 ## Overview
-Gaussian Initialization and Warm-up establishes the initial state of 3D Gaussian primitives for radiance field optimization, addressing the instability caused by poor starting positions and incorrect reflectivity assumptions. It sets apart from naive random placement by combining frustum-bounded randomization with a low-reflectivity bias and a static warm-up phase, preventing Gaussians from converging to incorrect local minima in low-reflectivity regions [S1]. Unlike dense initialization strategies that pre-compute millions of primitives, this method starts sparse and relies on subsequent densification or refinement [S1].
+Gaussian Initialization and Warm-up establishes the initial state of 3D Gaussian primitives and optimizes them in a static phase to ensure stable convergence in differentiable volume rendering. It solves the problem of poor initial placement and gradient instability, particularly in low-reflectivity regions or complex dynamic scenes, by biasing initial parameters and decoupling geometry optimization from dynamic deformation [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard sparse initialization by explicitly addressing the inverse-square falloff artifacts in low-reflectivity areas and using a static warm-up phase to place Gaussians before introducing complexity [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Camera frustum bounds (near/far planes), optional color images, raw C-ToF images or phasors, camera poses.
-**Outputs:** Initial set of 3D Gaussians with positions, covariances, opacities, and colors.
+The method combines geometric placement, parameter biasing, and a static optimization phase. It is primarily defined by the C-ToF dynamic radiance field approach [S1], with specific matrix initialization techniques from multi-grid scene representation networks [S2].
 
