+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+- **High Initial Reflectivity**: Setting initial reflectivity to high values (e.g., 0.5) causes Gaussians in low-reflectivity regions to be placed incorrectly far from the camera due to inverse-square falloff, leading to multiple opacity peaks and interference [S1].
+- **Background Saturation**: In low-reflectivity areas, if the background signal is too accurate, Gaussians may fail to place on surfaces. Random perturbation of the background signal prevents this [S1].
+- **Zero Initialization in Transformation Matrices**: Initializing transformation matrix entries to zero prevents gradients from updating them. Sampling from $\mathcal{N}(0, 0.05)$ ensures non-zero entries [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Dense Initialization (DI)](10026-dense-initialization-di.md)
-Dense Initialization (DI) is a Gaussian initialization strategy that generates millions of small Gaussians to densely cover all visible surfaces at the outset, rather than starting with a sparse set and progressively refining it. It solves the problem of slow convergence and the computational overhead of iterative densification stages used in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting. DI sets itself apart from its sibling, Recurrent Gaussian Update, by eliminating the need for densification entirely through high-density matching, which also benefits ray tracing performance by reducing the workload of irrelevant primitives.
+Dense Initialization (DI) generates millions of small Gaussians to densely cover all visible surfaces at the outset, rather than starting with a sparse set and progressively refining it. It solves the problem of slow convergence and the computational overhead of iterative densification stages used in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting. DI sets itself apart from its sibling, Recurrent Gaussian Update, by eliminating the need for densification entirely through high-density matching, which also benefits ray tracing performance by reducing the workload of irrelevant primitives.
 
 ### [Recurrent Gaussian Update](10974-recurrent-gaussian-update.md)
 Recurrent Gaussian Update is a gradient-free, feed-forward refinement method that iteratively corrects initial 3D Gaussian predictions by predicting incremental updates to Gaussian parameters and hidden states based on rendering errors from input views. It sets itself apart from standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) optimization by eliminating gradient computation, achieving a 53× speedup in reconstruction time and converging in 4 iterations instead of 30,000. Unlike single-step feed-forward models, it uses a recurrent error-correction loop to achieve higher parameter efficiency, where a smaller recurrent model outperforms significantly larger single-step models.
+
+### [Structure-Aware Initialization](13704-structure-aware-initialization.md)
+Structure-Aware Initialization is a strategy that adapts primitive placement and scale to local image complexity using local variance. It solves the problem of inefficient coverage in radiance-field optimization by ensuring dense, fine-grained primitives in complex regions and sparse, coarse coverage in smooth areas. Unlike Dense Initialization, which distributes primitives uniformly, this method accelerates convergence and improves final fidelity, particularly when using high primitive counts.
 
